From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 08:11:02 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] drm/apple: take an attached external display through a deep
 sleep

The external T8140 DCP refused every deep sleep with a display attached
("a display is attached, refusing sleep"), since nothing brought the
display back after the wake.

macOS releases the display at system sleep and replays the port's HPD at
the wake. In the 25G83 kernel, read statically,
IODPSwitch::handleSystemWillSleep() calls
AppleATCDPNativePort::handleDPSwitchWillSleep(), which logs "releasing
display..." and calls displayRelease() for a requested display; its
handleDPSwitchWillWake() calls checkInitialHPDState(), which synthesizes
the plug from the current HPD. The crossbar driver's strings suggest
that the DCPEXT firmware answers a release with Deactivate, after which
the crossbar connection goes down.

Do the same. Linux already releases the display the way DPMS off does
(pipe off, HPD low, DisplayRelease), and KDE turns the display off
before the sleep anyway. Then park the route's crossbar output and stop
the firmware as with no display. At the wake, after the firmware's warm
restart, select the crossbar output again; the Type-C work connects the
link as for a sleep with the CRTC off, and the CRTC is lit on it by DPMS
on, or by the DRM resume when it was on. The Type-C route and the
connector stay throughout, so the compositor keeps its output and its
framebuffers. A display unplugged or replugged during the sleep goes
through the port controller's revalidation after the wake, as any
unplug or plug does.

Measured on a J700: an external USB-C display attached through a sleep
is released for it and lights again after the wake.

Signed-off-by: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>

[j604 backport: applied clean. Callers are T8140 DCPEXT sleep paths only. Aurora's message cites a static read of a macOS kernel for the release/HPD-replay behaviour; no Apple code was read for this port, which uses only the code structure]
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5

---
 drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp.c | 27 ++++++++++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp.c
index 667ece8c2e..466eb4011d 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp.c
@@ -7436,8 +7436,12 @@ static int dcp_s2r_stop_firmware(struct apple_dcp *dcp)
 /*
  * macOS stops this firmware with one call the size of A500, then about 4 ms later AP
  * then IOP idle and the run bit off twice. Nothing has shown it needing a secure
- * port. Its panel path is already off with no display attached. Nothing brings it
- * back yet, so an attached display refuses the sleep.
+ * port.
+ *
+ * An attached display goes first, as macOS's DP port releases its display at system
+ * sleep: the pipe off, HPD low and the display released, as DPMS off does, then the
+ * crossbar output. The route and the connector stay, and after the wake the link
+ * connects again, as macOS replays the port's HPD.
  */
 static bool dcp_s2r_ext_capable(struct apple_dcp *dcp)
 {
@@ -7533,9 +7537,12 @@ static int dcp_s2r_ext_suspend(struct apple_dcp *dcp)
 		dcp_s2r_stop_failed_start(dcp);
 		dcp_s2r_find_darts(dcp);
 		dcp->s2r_stopped = true;
+		dcp_typec_route_park(dcp);
 		ret = dcp_s2r_stop_streams(dcp);
-		if (ret)
+		if (ret) {
+			dcp_typec_route_unpark(dcp);
 			return ret == -ETIMEDOUT ? -EBUSY : ret;
+		}
 		dcp_pmc_forget(dcp);
 		device_set_wakeup_path(dcp->dev);
 		return 0;
@@ -7550,10 +7557,8 @@ static int dcp_s2r_ext_suspend(struct apple_dcp *dcp)
 	if (READ_ONCE(dcp->crashed) || IS_ERR_OR_NULL(dcp->rtk))
 		return -EBUSY;
 
-	if (READ_ONCE(dcp->active_typec_route) || READ_ONCE(dcp->typec_cable_connected)) {
-		dev_err(dcp->dev, "a display is attached, refusing sleep\n");
-		return -EBUSY;
-	}
+	if (READ_ONCE(dcp->active_typec_route) || READ_ONCE(dcp->typec_cable_connected))
+		dev_dbg(dcp->dev, "a display is attached: released for the sleep, connected again after it\n");
 
 	dcp_disable_typec_work(dcp, false);
 	ret = dcp_external_disconnect(dcp, false);
@@ -7572,10 +7577,14 @@ static int dcp_s2r_ext_suspend(struct apple_dcp *dcp)
 		return -EBUSY;
 	}
 
+	/* The display's link went above: its crossbar output follows, as on macOS. */
+	dcp_typec_route_park(dcp);
+
 	ret = iomfb_s2r_500_h17p(dcp);
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err(dcp->dev, "no reply to A500: %d\n", ret);
 		WRITE_ONCE(dcp->crashed, true);
+		dcp_typec_route_unpark(dcp);
 		return ret == -ETIMEDOUT ? -EBUSY : ret;
 	}
 	usleep_range(4000, 5000);
@@ -7586,6 +7595,7 @@ static int dcp_s2r_ext_suspend(struct apple_dcp *dcp)
 			readl(dcp->coproc_reg + APPLE_DCP_COPROC_CPU_CONTROL),
 			readl(dcp->coproc_reg + APPLE_DCP_COPROC_CPU_STATUS));
 		WRITE_ONCE(dcp->crashed, true);
+		dcp_typec_route_unpark(dcp);
 		return ret == -ETIMEDOUT ? -EBUSY : ret;
 	}
 
@@ -8082,6 +8092,9 @@ static int dcp_s2r_restart(struct apple_dcp *dcp, bool real)
 			dev_err(dcp->dev, "warm restart failed: %d\n", ret);
 	}
 	dcp_s2r_put_darts(dcp);
+	/* An attached display's crossbar output; its link reconnects from the Type-C work. */
+	if (dcp_s2r_ext_capable(dcp))
+		dcp_typec_route_unpark(dcp);
 	if (ret) {
 		WRITE_ONCE(dcp->crashed, true);
 		dev_warn(dcp->dev, "firmware stopped for sleep, display stays off until reboot\n");
